{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/session-18868900-demo-budget.md", "content": "# Heterogeneous demos: transfer and stopping audit

This session supplied four sharply different examples: a 28×14 input yielding a singleton color, two large periodic/lattice inputs yielding compact 4×6 and 5×3 grids, and a 22×27 lattice yielding 5×3. Their output shapes and palette encodings differ, while the 27×27 test is a periodic 3/4/9/2/7/1 lattice. The singleton output was not a valid basis for the test shape, and a guessed 6×4 block expansion was rejected. These examples demonstrate that output shape must be inferred independently from palette/marker structure and must replay across relevant demos before submission.

Reusable audit: (1) record input and output dimensions and marker coordinates for every pair; (2) distinguish output-shape rule from color/phase rule; (3) normalize geometry and role colors, then require a single candidate to reproduce complete demos; (4) after rejection, reconsider shape and mapping together rather than making a cosmetic variant; (5) request another demo only if an explicit ambiguity remains and the next pair could distinguish candidate outputs. This session did not establish a validated transformation, so do not treat any proposed test output as a known answer.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/seen/play/users/u094/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/session-18868900-demo-budget.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/heterogeneous-demo-turn-selection.md", "content": "# Selecting demos for heterogeneous ARC examples

When available demonstrations differ sharply in grid size, palette, and object structure, log each pair's role colors, exact changed-cell mask, untouched/identity regions, and any plausible structural trigger. Treat a rule as supported only if it reproduces complete outputs, not merely the salient changed motif.

Before requesting another pair, name the competing hypotheses and the concrete observation that would distinguish them; ask only if that distinction can materially change the test prediction. Repeated requests without a targeted discriminator have cost but may not improve inference. In the same way, do not progress from one vivid example directly to a large speculative output: preserve the test input unless evidence supports a transformation. Keep this evidence-gathering discipline compatible with strict interfaces: each puzzle response still emits exactly one action JSON.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/seen/play/users/u094/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/heterogeneous-demo-turn-selection.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/periodic-stripe-sequence.md", "content": "# Periodic stripe grids: global-sequence hypothesis

When demos are made of uniform-color rows or columns, reason over the 1D stripe sequence as well as individual cells. Compare the input stripe sequence with the full output sequence, including repeated periods and boundary phase. A transformation may generate a periodic color sequence from a compact set of stripe exemplars rather than preserve or locally extend the visible rows. Extract the exact sequence from every demo, test candidate periods and phase, and verify every output stripe before transferring to the test. Do not extrapolate from a single visually suggestive sample; request another demo if the rule connecting input stripe order to output period remains ambiguous.
