{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/task-18868900-shape-and-transfer-audit.md", "content": "# Output-shape transfer and heterogeneous demonstrations

This instance combined highly dissimilar examples: a periodic grid returned a small 2×2 result, while other lattice-like examples returned singleton colors. The test was a large periodic stripe lattice with several divider rows/columns and sparse marker colors. A guessed compact 4×4 pattern and then a singleton marker-color output were both rejected. These rejections do not identify the correct rule; they show that matching output size or marker palette by superficial resemblance is insufficient.

Reusable check: maintain independent hypotheses for (a) structural trigger, (b) output dimensions, and (c) output color/phase. Replay each complete demo against the hypothesized operation. When geometries differ substantially, compare functional roles and transformed masks; do not assume a common mapping merely because all examples have stripes or divider lines. Before another demo request, specify which competing test predictions it would distinguish. After rejection, do not make an unsupported shape/color variant; change the model only with evidence, and keep strict one-action JSON serialization.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/seen/play/users/u094/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/task-18868900-shape-and-transfer-audit.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/session-18868900-output-shape-review.md", "content": "# Session review: periodic lattice to compact output

This session is a cautionary example of under-validated transfer. Demos supplied repetitive stripe/lattice grids with compact outputs of several different dimensions (5×6, 2×4, 5×4, 3×2), while the test had a 28×27 periodic lattice. A small guessed grid based on salient marker cells was submitted without a demonstrated derivation of its dimensions or cell mapping; it was incorrect. A second answer likewise used an unvalidated extraction rule and was incorrect.

Reusable lesson: before transferring a periodic or lattice transformation, explicitly derive both (a) output dimensions and (b) the mapping from complete input units/coordinates to output cells, using every demo. Do not confuse a visually plausible marker subset with the actual extraction rule. After binary rejection, it is not enough to alter one edge or color: reassess shape and mapping together, and request a demo only when a specific competing mapping can be distinguished.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/seen/play/users/u094/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/session-18868900-output-shape-review.md"}
